<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porta Pia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porta Pia is a gate in the Aurelian Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-iv">Pope Pius IV</a> and built in the 1560s. On 20 September 1870 Italian troops entered Rome through a breach in the walls beside it, ending the temporal power of the popes, and the Capture of Rome is often called simply 'Porta Pia'. Rome became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porta-pia.jpg" alt="Porta Pia" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monticiano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porta_Pia_-_internal_side.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
